              h: ['*At Lamentations 1:18, Jerusalem admits that Jehovah is righteous and that she rebelled against him.', 'Lamentations 2:19 tells the people to pour out their hearts to Jehovah like water.', 'Jeremiah said his eyes were worn out from tears, Lamentations 2:11, so he really cared about his people.', 'Lamentations 1:16 shows how lonely it feels when no one comforts us, so we can be comforters for others.'],
              x: ['*Lamentations 1:18 has Jerusalem admit that Jehovah is righteous and the fault was hers. Blaming God for trouble we bring on ourselves is foolish, Proverbs 19:3. Honest confession, like David’s at Psalm 51:3, 4, opens the way to mercy.', 'Chapters 1 to 4 of Lamentations are acrostics, with verses starting with successive letters of the Hebrew alphabet. Jeremiah expressed deep grief in a carefully crafted form, perhaps to make it easy to remember. Strong feelings can still be expressed in an orderly way.', 'Lamentations 2:11 shows Jeremiah weeping over his people even though many of them had mistreated him. Jesus likewise wept over Jerusalem at Luke 19:41. Like them, we can feel compassion for people who reject our message rather than resentment.', 'Lamentations 2:19 urges the people to rise in the night and pour out their hearts like water before Jehovah. Water poured out is not held back, so the picture suggests telling Jehovah everything. Psalm 62:8 invites us to pray that same way.']
